# <%= class_name %> account-data integration

Status: draft. Generated code fails explicitly until transport and normalization
are implemented. Generating this package does not make it available for connection
or sync. The shared persistence/runtime rollout is described in
[the architecture](../architecture/bank-data-providers.md).

## Configuration

The declared fields have `<%= options[:credential_scope] %>` scope. Fields marked
secret belong in encrypted credential storage; other fields belong in validated
settings. Defaults are configuration values, never credentials. All connections
remain family-owned, even with application credentials. A provider with both
application credentials and connection tokens must model both before activation.

## Implement and verify

1. Implement `Client` with the provider's documented API or compose an existing SDK.
   Inject credentials; bound requests, timeouts and retries; sanitize errors. Rotate
   tokens through the shared credential owner under its write fence.
2. Implement adapter methods returning `Provider::AccountData::Page` containing
   `Ingestion::Record.account`, `Ingestion::Record.transaction`<% if options[:type] == "investment" %>, `Ingestion::Record.holding` and `Ingestion::Record.activity`<% end %> values.
   Records carry decimals, explicit currencies and dates. Do not write to the ledger,
   create jobs or advance persisted cursors inside the adapter.
3. Replace every failing generated test with assertions over sanitized fixtures/VCR.
   Verify partial/empty snapshots, pagination, rate limits, authentication failures,
   pending-to-posted identity, removed IDs, signs and currency/FX semantics.
4. Define actual API scope/coverage and whether each resource is a snapshot or delta.
   No missing data implies deletion. Return recoverable warnings without raw payloads;
   the shared runtime captures support incidents through `DebugLogEntry.capture`.
   A connection-wide transaction log overrides `transaction_scope` with `:connection`
   and implements `fetch_transaction_group` using `TransactionGroup`; its cursor
   must not be passed to the ordinary per-account page runner. Follow the
   [generation protocol](../architecture/connection-change-sets.md), including
   complete capture, restart, bounded account publication and cursor promotion.
<% if options[:type] == "investment" -%>
   A connection-wide activity log instead overrides `activity_scope` with
   `:connection` and implements `fetch_activity_group` with resource `activities`.
   Opt into `resumable_activity_groups?` only when retained prefixes and credential
   transitions support recovery in the same logical Sync. Activity groups cannot
   authorize removals or holdings absence.
<% end -%>
5. Complete the runtime activation gates in the architecture before changing
   `native_ready?` to true. The registry discovers adapter declarations from the
   application source tree; discovery does not authorize a legacy cutover.
   For a legacy integration, preserve its `source` and external IDs and complete its
   row in the [migration matrix](../architecture/bank-data-provider-migration-matrix.md).
6. Review the [shared account setup contract](../architecture/provider-native-account-setup.md)
   before declaring `self.account_setup_types` (disabled by default). Declare all
   required source-policy resources, including historical balances when applicable.
   Verify initially unlinked observations and copied caches can be replayed under
   a new authorized link without rewriting their original evidence. Shared setup
   preserves existing authority when adding a secondary source; it does not merge
   overlapping financial history or switch writers.

Run `bin/rails test test/models/provider/account_data/<%= file_name %>_test.rb`, then
the repository's required checks. A green scaffold identity test alone is not an
implementation or a migration acceptance test.
